<h2>The provision in outline</h2>
<p>Section 148A was inserted in 1976 to answer a recurring injustice: interim orders obtained ex parte by a party who knew perfectly well who the contesting respondent would be. The section allows a person claiming a right to appear to lodge a caveat in respect of an application "expected to be made" in a proceeding "about to be instituted". The duties it then creates are symmetrical — the caveator must notify the expected applicant that a caveat exists; the court must notify the caveator when the application arrives; and the applicant must supply the caveator with the papers.</p>

<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>1. Anticipate the forum.</strong> Identify where the expected application will be filed — the appellate court for a stay application, the commercial court for an injunction, the executing court for objections.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>2. Lodge the caveat.</strong> File the caveat petition in that court, describing the expected proceeding and the caveator\'s interest, with the prescribed court fee and an affidavit where required by practice.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>3. Serve the expected applicant.</strong> Dispatch notice of the caveat by registered post to the person expected to move the court — a statutory duty on the caveator.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>4. Watch the ninety days.</strong> Diarise the expiry and re-lodge if the threat persists; an expired caveat protects nothing.</div>
</div>

<h2>Where caveats earn their keep in Delhi practice</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><h3>After a decree or final order</h3><p>The winner of a suit or petition expects an appeal — an RFA, FAO or LPA — accompanied by a stay application. A caveat in the appellate court ensures the stay is not granted unheard.</p></div>
  <div class="tile"><h3>Around arbitral awards</h3><p>An award-holder anticipating a Section 34 challenge with an application to stay enforcement lodges a caveat so that any interim order on the stay application is passed only after hearing it.</p></div>
  <div class="tile"><h3>Property and possession</h3><p>A party in possession, or one who has lawfully re-entered, expects an injunction suit; a caveat in the district court forestalls an ex parte status quo order that could take months to vacate.</p></div>
  <div class="tile"><h3>Commercial terminations</h3><p>After terminating a dealership, franchise or construction contract, the terminating party can expect an urgent injunction application — a caveat converts the first hearing from an ambush into a contested one.</p></div>
</div>

<h2>What a caveat does not do</h2>
<p>A caveat secures a hearing, nothing more. It is not an advance defence, does not require the court to refuse interim relief, and does not entitle the caveator to delay the hearing — a caveator who is served and fails to appear can be proceeded against ex parte quite lawfully. Nor does the lapse of notice formalities invariably invalidate an order: an interim order passed in ignorance of a subsisting caveat is an irregularity the caveator must promptly bring to the court\'s attention, typically by an application to recall the order. The caveat, in short, is an early-warning system, and like every such system it works only when backed by readiness — counsel briefed, papers assembled, and appearance entered the moment notice arrives.</p>

<h2>Drafting and filing points</h2>
<div class="check">
  <p>Describe the expected proceeding broadly enough to catch it however it is framed — naming the parties, the subject matter, and the order impugned or the dispute concerned — because a caveat tied too narrowly to one case type can miss the application actually filed. Give complete addresses, including email and counsel\'s particulars, so Registry notice actually reaches the caveator in time. In the Delhi High Court, caveats are lodged through the Registry (including by e-filing) against expected proceedings on both the original and appellate sides; in the district courts, the caveat is filed in the court concerned. Keep proof of dispatch of the caveator\'s own notice to the expected applicant — it answers any later suggestion that the caveat was lodged in secret.</p>
</div>

<div class="note">
  <p><strong>Practice note:</strong> the caveat is among the cheapest forms of litigation insurance available to a Delhi litigant — a single filing, renewed every ninety days while the risk lasts, against the cost of dislodging an ex parte order already acted upon. This article is for general information only and is not legal advice.</p>
</div>
